  "id": "Compound::DB01185 - Gene::367",
  "verbalization": "Fluoxymesterone, an androgenic steroid, is implicated in the pathogenesis of androgenetic alopecia, enlarged clitoris, and peliosis hepatis, all of which are mediated through Oxandrolone, an anabolic steroid. The shared mechanism involves Oxandrolone binding to Ar, a receptor that is central to the biological effects of anabolic steroids. This binding is a critical step in the cascade of events that lead to the observed clinical manifestations. The evidence from the paths suggests that Fluoxymesterone and Oxandrolone, both anabolic steroids, share a common pathway involving Ar, which is a key target for these compounds. The low common ancestor of Fluoxymesterone and Oxandrolone, both belonging to the 17Beta-hydroxy steroid class, further supports the notion of a shared biological mechanism. The integrated evidence points to a mechanistic link between Fluoxymesterone and Ar, mediated through Oxandrolone, highlighting the role of Ar in the pathophysiology of these conditions. The findings underscore the importance of targeting Ar as a potential therapeutic strategy, particularly in the context of anabolic steroid-induced disorders.",
